# docs/canvas-plan.md §7. THE THREE VERBS ARE NOT REDUNDANT: the hash
# proves this libkaya made the same pixels as every other's and NOTHING
# about the screen, expect_drawing is the legible one, and expect_ink is
# the only one that fails when the BLIT dropped. The hash can be ONE
# STRING because every input is fixed and all five lanes are aarch64
# (docs/measurements/canvas-cross-isa-2026-08-26.txt).
expect_title "canvas"
expect label#0 "portfolio value"
# THE FIGURE IS INSET INSIDE ITS VIEWBOX, so these four numbers can
# discriminate at all: a drawing that filled its box reads 0,0,100,100
# whatever the transform did.
expect_drawing canvas@chart "41/2,8,97,83"
expect_drawing_hash canvas@chart "7ae0a9280909ee7b"
# THE BLIT, sampled at flat-region CENTRES (a rescale blurs a boundary
# into a colour no palette entry has) and in BOTH appearance modes,
# since a light-only string is green on a light machine and red on a
# dark one. FREEZE FROM THE CORE — canvas.rs's
# the_scene_probe_points_are_opaque_and_pinned prints both — never from
# a platform read; §7.2 allows ±1 for the macOS backing store's profile.
expect_ink canvas@chart "15,20 70,63 = light FFFFFF/D2E3F7 dark 16181C/212A35"
# A drawing is an IMAGE to every accessibility tree and the app authors
# the spoken name (§9): nothing builds a semantics tree out of the ops.
expect_ax canvas@chart "image/Portfolio value"
# The direction paints (docs/looks-plan.md §3.3): four bars, up, its fill, down,
# its fill, each probed at its centre; the fills composite over the palette's
# ground. FROZEN FROM THE CORE, as above (the_trend_probe_points_are_pinned).
expect_ink canvas@trend "12,50 37,50 62,50 87,50 = light 209D55/D2EBDD/D93A2B/F7D8D5 dark 4FD37A/1D2F27/FF6B5E/322324"
